<article class="env-lemma" id="0GWF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GWF">Lemma <span data-tag="0GWF">57.10.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category with enough negative objects. Let $X \in D^ b(\mathcal{A})$. Let $b \in \mathbf{Z}$ with $H^ i(X) = 0$ for $i > b$. Then there exists a map $N[-b] \to X$ such that the induced map $N \to H^ b(X)$ is surjective and $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (H^ b(X), N) = 0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using the truncation functors we can represent $X$ by a complex $A^ a \to A^{a + 1} \to \ldots \to A^ b$ of objects of $\mathcal{A}$. Choose $N$ in $\mathcal{A}$ such that there exists a surjection $t : N \to A^ b$ and such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (A^ b, N) = 0$. Then the surjection $t$ defines a map $N[-b] \to X$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>